Falsifies `paging::x86_64::no_vtd_entry_ever_sets_a_reserved_bit`.

The defect: `VTD_ADDR_MASK` is widened from bits 51:12 to bits 62:12, so
`Vtd::leaf_entry` and `Vtd::table_entry` carry address bits into a range a VT-d
second-level entry reserves. The constant's own doc comment says this is a live
parameter rather than a hypothetical one: VT-d's real address width is
`CAP_REG.MGAW`-defined and this driver has never narrowed to it, so the mask is
a number somebody could plausibly change. The failure mode is a confined device
whose every translated DMA faults on a reserved bit, which presents as broken
hardware rather than as a bad table.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(leaf & !VTD_PERMITTED_BITS, 0)`, the first of the
harness's assertions, and the `table_entry` one below it for the same reason.
Measured on patagonia 2026-09-16: FAILED, 1 of 68 checks.

**This defect replaced an earlier one, and the replacement is the whole of
milestone 307's finding here.** The patch recorded until 2026-09-16 set the
Accessed bit in `Vtd::leaf_entry` (`let mut bits = VTD_R | A`), copying
`Ia32e::attrs` two hundred lines up; bit 8 is defined in an IA-32e entry and
reserved in a VT-d one. That defect is real and the harness did catch it, but it
is a defect in the *flag* half, and the flag half was the only half the harness
could see. Every assertion, and the `kani::assume` above them, was spelled
through `VTD_ADDR_MASK`, `VTD_R` and `VTD_W`, which are the three constants the
encoder builds the entry out of, so `(pa & M) | bits` sets no bit outside
`M | VTD_R | VTD_W` for every value of M, and the assume admitted exactly the
addresses M allowed. The address half of row 12's claim was a tautology.

Measured both ways, same machine and day, with this patch applied:

  - the pre-307 harness: **SUCCESSFUL, 0 of 45 failed. A survivor.**
  - the harness as it now stands: FAILED, 1 of 68.

So this patch is recorded rather than the Accessed-bit one because it is the
defect the harness was blind to, and a record whose job is saying what is known
should carry the harder case. The superseded defect is written out above instead
of kept, because two patches cannot share one §134 path and the one worth
replaying is this one.

The host twin `a_vtd_leaf_sets_no_bit_outside_read_write_and_address` catches
this too, in microseconds, but only since it was given a physical address with
bits above 51 in it: with its old single concrete `0x10_0000` the encoder's
masking was never exercised, so a wider mask changed nothing it could observe.
That is the same blindness one level down, and it is why this patch is not
sufficient evidence on its own that the property is watched.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
@@ -209,7 +209,7 @@
 /// width `Ia32e::ADDR_MASK` uses, because nothing this kernel runs names a wider physical address
 /// yet; VT-d's actual width is `CAP_REG.MGAW`-defined and this driver has not needed to narrow to
 /// it (`kernel/src/arch/x86_64/iommu.rs`'s BUGS says why).
-const VTD_ADDR_MASK: u64 = 0x000f_ffff_ffff_f000;
+const VTD_ADDR_MASK: u64 = 0x7fff_ffff_ffff_f000;
 
 /// **Every bit a VT-d second-level entry may carry**: R (bit 0), W (bit 1) and the address field
 /// (bits 51:12), per QEMU's `VTD_SPTE_PAGE_L1_RSVD_MASK`; any other bit faults the transaction.
